  "title": "Week 6: Life as a Miner in Krakow",
  "date": "2026-02-15T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Week 6: Life as a Miner in Krakow\n\nI arrived in Krakow on Valentine’s Day to a heavy blanket of snow ❄️. Since it was a Saturday, I had to rush for groceries—in Poland, the Sunday trading ban means almost everything closes except for tiny kiosks.🛒🏃‍♂️\n\nA Taste of Home in Poland 🥟\n\nThe local food reminds me so much of Tianjin style: plenty of boiled carbohydrates! It felt like a warm hug in the cold weather. 😋\n\n| Pierogi | Pampuchy | Faworki |\n|-|-|-|\n| Pierogi| Pampuchy | Faworki|\n\nExploring the City 🚋\n\nKrakow feels more modernized than many other European cities I've visited. Their transport hubs are true \"market centers\"—the main train station is literally inside a massive shopping mall! 🛍️ I'm staying right in the sweet spot between the station and the Old Town.\n\nBazylika Mariacka\n\nAt the top of St. Mary's Basilica (Bazylika Mariacka), a trumpeter plays every hour 🎺. You’ll notice the music stops abruptly in the middle. This honors a 13th-century trumpeter who was shot in the throat by an arrow while sounding the alarm during a Mongolian invasion. A haunting but beautiful tradition. 🏹\n\nDeep Dive: The Wieliczka Salt Mine 🧂⚒️\n\nThe Wieliczka Salt Mine is hundreds of years old and was once a primary \"money pillar\" for the Polish Kingdom. I booked the Miner’s Route, which was an amazing hands-on experience! I actually got to mine salt and learn about the grueling daily lives of the historical miners.\n\nselfie\n\nFun Fact: Did you know the word \"Salary\" comes from \"Salt\"? 😲 In Roman times, soldiers were often paid in salt. Here is my \"salary\" for the day! 👇\n\nmy salary\n\ninside the mine\n\nPro-tip: Because the train station is hidden inside the shopping center, I actually got lost and missed my first train to the mine! 😅 Luckily, the tickets are flexible for two hours, so I caught the next one.\n\nThe train station in „Wieliczka”\n\nWawel Castle & The Dragon 🐉🏰\n\nBy the time I reached the Castle, it was too late to go inside, but the exterior walk was stunning. Wawel Cathedral is a beautiful \"chaos\" of design—it wasn't built all at once, so you can see different architectural styles clashing across the centuries. 🏗️\n\nI noticed most rooftops here are a distinct green. Originally, these were iron/copper and turned green through oxidation. Eventually, this became a status symbol! People began using pre-oxidized materials just to show off how \"old\" and expensive their buildings were. ✨\n\nWawel Cathedral\n\nFinally, I visited the Wawel Dragon sculpture. Legend says a dragon used to eat the locals until a clever man fed it a lamb skin stuffed with sulfur. The dragon ate it, got so thirsty he drank half the Vistula River, and exploded! 💥\n\nDragon\n\nThe sculpture usually spits real fire every few minutes, but I think it was too cold today—the dragon stayed quiet. 🥶🔥\n\nFun tip: 胡芦娃(Kurwa) is a curse word in Polish.",
